 "rules": {
  "displayOnly": "These indicators are display-only evidence. They are never used in any score or ranking.",
  "modis": "MODIS MOD21A2 v061 (Terra, 1 km). Band-29 emissivity e29 at 8.55 µm: quartz and sulfate (gypsum) have strong emissivity lows (reststrahlen bands) near 8.5-9 µm, so e29 is low on quartz- or sulfate-rich surfaces and high on low-silica rock (basalt), clay, carbonate, vegetation and water. Bands 31 (11 µm) and 32 (12 µm) are stored for reference; they stay near 0.96-0.98 on most land. Site value: mean of all valid pixels of the 3 x 3 pixel window (about 2.8 km x 2.8 km) around the site over all 138 Terra 8-day composites of 2018-2020 (46 per year); samples with daytime LST missing or below 0 °C are left out (snow, ice or frozen ground raise emissivity). n = number of pixel samples (pixels x composites).",
  "s2": {
   "feRatio": "Iron-oxide ratio B4/B2 (red / blue surface reflectance): ferric iron oxides (hematite, goethite) absorb blue light, so a higher ratio means a redder, more iron-stained surface (van der Meer et al. 2014).",
   "swirRatio": "2.2 µm ratio B11/B12 (1.6 / 2.2 µm): clay (Al-OH), carbonate and sulfate minerals absorb in the B12 range (about 2.1-2.3 µm), so a higher ratio means more of these minerals (van der Meer et al. 2014).",
   "brightness": "Unweighted mean of B2, B3, B4, B8, B11 and B12 surface reflectance (0-1), a broadband-like brightness (not a calibrated albedo): dark basalt and varnished rock are low; salt, gypsum, chalk and sand are high.",
   "ndvi": "NDVI = (B8 - B4) / (B8 + B4); flags vegetation. When NDVI at the site is above 0.3 in any used scene, the site is not interpreted.",
   "method": "3 x 3 pixels of 20 m (60 m) around the site; the 10 m bands are averaged to 20 m first; only pixels that the L2A scene classification (SCL) calls bare (5) or vegetation (4) are used, and a scene needs 5 of 9. Per scene the median of the pixels; site value = median of the scene values. Scenes from 2023-01-01 to 2024-12-31: clearest first (cloud cover <= 5 %, the clearest scene of each season first, then up to 20 %); the first 3 usable scenes are kept. Reflectance = DN x 0.0001 (Earth Search COGs with boa_offset_applied = true; the offset is not applied again). waterPct = largest share of water (SCL 6) in the ~2.8 km MODIS window among the used scenes."
  },
  "aster": "ASTER L1T thermal bands 10-14 (90 m, 2000-2006). Clear daytime scenes (cloud cover <= 5 %, descending = daytime pass, sun elevation > 20°) that contain the site; the first 6 by (cloud cover, date) are read; 5 x 5 pixels (about 450 m) around the site. Radiance L = (DN - 1) x unit conversion coefficient; emissivity e10..e14 by the normalized emissivity method (maximum emissivity 0.97). Silica index = (e13 + e14) / 2 - (e10 + e12) / 2, from the median of each band's emissivity over all valid pixels of all used scenes: silica minerals pull bands 10-12 (8.1-9.3 µm) down while bands 13-14 stay high, so a larger index means a more silica-rich surface. quartzIndex = L11² / (L10 x L12) (Ninomiya et al. 2005). Not atmospherically corrected — relative only: water vapour also lowers bands 10-12, so compare sites with each other, not with laboratory values. rank = position from the highest silica index among the sites with ASTER data.",
  "reading": "From the MODIS band-29 mean e29 only: e29 < 0.88 → 'quartz- or sulfate-rich surface'; e29 > 0.95 → 'low-silica surface (basalt-like, clay or carbonate)'; otherwise 'mixed'. Not interpreted when Sentinel-2 shows vegetation (NDVI > 0.3) or open water at the site. A reading describes the top surface (thermal bands see the top fraction of a millimetre); it never names a rock.",
  "thresholds": "Physics: quartz- and sulfate-rich materials have deep emissivity lows at 8-9.5 µm, while basalt, carbonate, clay, vegetation and water stay high there (laboratory spectra: Salisbury & D'Aria 1992; global ASTER emissivity: Hulley et al. 2015; MODIS band-29 emissivity from the same temperature-emissivity separation: Gillespie et al. 1998, Hulley & Hook 2011). The cut values 0.88 and 0.95 are ours, placed between those ranges; we looked at these same 19 sites and the scout's 2023 values when fixing them, so the readings and the Jordan checks are illustrations, not independent tests.",
  "jordanCheck": "Only for the 5 Jordan sites: the reading is compared with the app's rock type (data/sites_meta.json geology.primary). Expected reading: sandstone → quartz- or sulfate-rich; basalt and carbonate → low-silica; mudstone → low-silica or mixed; evaporite → quartz- or sulfate-rich (gypsum) or low-silica (halite, aragonite, clay), so for evaporite the check is weak. 'agrees' = the reading is the expected one; 'partly' = the reading is 'mixed', or it is silica-rich over a mapped carbonate or mudstone (thermal bands see only the top surface, and in deserts a lag of chert (flint) gravel or blown quartz sand often covers soft sedimentary rock); 'differs' = otherwise. The reason quotes e29 and, when available, the ASTER silica index and its rank among the sites.",
  "flags": {
   "vegetation": "Sentinel-2 NDVI > 0.3 at the site in at least one used scene: not interpreted.",
   "water": "the site point is open water in Sentinel-2's scene classification: not interpreted.",
   "waterNearby": "water covers more than 20 % of the ~2.8 km MODIS window in at least one used Sentinel-2 scene; water raises e29, so read e29 with care.",
   "coldSeason": "more than 25 % of the valid MODIS samples were left out because daytime LST was below 0 °C: the mean is from the snow-free season only.",
   "vegetationUnchecked": "no usable Sentinel-2 scene, so vegetation could not be checked.",
   "oneS2Scene": "only one usable Sentinel-2 scene.",
   "oneAsterScene": "only one clear daytime ASTER scene.",
   "noModis": "no MODIS value (why given).",
   "noS2": "no Sentinel-2 value (why given).",
   "noAster": "no ASTER value (why given)."
  }
 },
